<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=dynamite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dynamite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dynamite is an explosive made of nitroglycerin, an absorbent such as clay or diatomaceous earth, and stabilisers, invented in 1866 by the Swedish chemist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>, who stabilised nitroglycerin with earth from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> near his factory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and patented in 1867. Nitroglycerin, first synthesised by Ascanio Sobrero in 1847, had been too sensitive to shock for commercial use. Dynamite was the first safely manageable explosive stronger than black powder and rapidly gained wide-scale use, though it was never popular in warfare; Nobel named it from the Greek dýnamis, &quot;power&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dynamite.jpg" alt="Dynamite" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olecrab · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caisse_dynamite_nobel_paulilles_expo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
